== Chapter 9

#align(center)[#smallcaps(all: true)[ALL THE PRINCIPLES OF PIETY SUBVERTED BY FANATICS, WHO SUBSTITUTE REVELATIONS FOR SCRIPTURE.]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Sections.]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[1\. The disposition and error of the Libertines, who claim for themselves the name of spiritual, briefly described. Their refutation. 1. The Apostles and all true Christians have embraced the written Word. This is confirmed by a passage in Isaiah, and also by the example and words of Paul. 2. The Spirit of Christ seals the doctrine of the written Word on the minds of the godly.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[2\. The refutation continued. 3. The deceptions of Satan cannot be detected without the help of the written Word. First objection. The answer to it.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[3\. Second objection, from the words of Paul about the #emph[letter and spirit]. The answer, with an explanation of Paul’s meaning. How the Spirit and the written Word are inseparably joined.]

#v(0.8em)

1\. #smallcaps[Those] who reject Scripture and imagine that they have some special way of reaching God should be regarded as held not so much by error as by madness. For certain flighty men#footnote[Lactantius: Cœlestes literas corruperunt, ut novam sibi doctrinam sine ulla radice ac stabilitate componerent \[They corrupted the heavenly writings, to put together for themselves a new doctrine without any root or stability\]. Vide Calvin in Instruct. adv. Libertinos, cap. 9 and 10. (Beveridge’s note.)] have lately appeared who, while they most haughtily lay claim to the teaching of the Spirit, themselves reject all reading of the Scriptures, and mock the simplicity of those who still delight in what they call the dead and deadly letter. But I would like them to tell me what spirit it is whose inspiration lifts them to such lofty heights that they dare to despise the doctrine of Scripture as lowly and childish. If they answer that it is the Spirit of Christ, their confidence is utterly ridiculous, since they will, I presume, admit that the apostles and the other believers in the early Church were enlightened by no other Spirit. Yet none of them learned from this to despise the word of God; instead, each was filled with greater reverence for it, as their writings most clearly testify. And indeed this had been foretold by the mouth of Isaiah (Isa 59:21). For when he says, “My Spirit who is on you, and My words that I have put in your mouth, will not depart from your mouth or from the mouths of your children and grandchildren, from now on and forevermore, says the #smallcaps[Lord],” he is not binding the ancient Church to outward doctrine as if it were a mere child learning its letters;#footnote[For the Latin “ac si elementarius esset,” the French has “comme s’ils eussent été petis enfans a l’A, B, C”—as if they were little children at their A, B, C. (Beveridge’s note.)] rather, he shows that under the reign of Christ the true and full blessedness of the new Church will consist in its being ruled no less by the Word than by the Spirit of God. From this we conclude that these scoundrels are guilty of a monstrous sacrilege when they tear apart what the prophet has joined in an unbreakable union. Add to this that Paul, though caught up even to the third heaven, did not cease to profit from the doctrine of the law and the prophets; and in the same way he urges Timothy, a teacher of outstanding excellence, to give attention to reading (1 Tim 4:13). And the praise he gives to Scripture well deserves to be remembered: namely, that it “is useful for instruction, for conviction, for correction, and for training in righteousness, so that the man of God may be perfect” (2 Tim 3:16). What devilish madness it is, then, to imagine that Scripture, which leads the sons of God all the way to the final goal, is of passing and temporary use! Again, I would like these people to tell me whether they have drunk in any other Spirit than the one Christ promised to his disciples. Though their madness is extreme, it will hardly carry them so far as to make this their boast. But what kind of Spirit did our Savior promise to send? One who would not speak on his own authority (John 16:13), but would bring to mind and instill the truths that Christ himself had delivered through the word. So the office of the Spirit promised to us is not to invent new and unheard-of revelations, or to coin a new kind of doctrine that would lead us away from the received doctrine of the gospel, but to seal on our minds the very doctrine that the gospel commends.

2\. From this it is easy to see that, if we want to gain any benefit from the Spirit of God, we must apply ourselves diligently both to reading and to hearing Scripture (just as Peter praises those who attentively study the doctrine of the prophets (2 Pet 1:19), though it might have been thought to be superseded once the light of the gospel had dawned). On the other hand, it is just as easy to see that any spirit which passes by the wisdom of God’s Word and puts forward some other doctrine is rightly suspected of emptiness and falsehood. Since Satan transforms himself into an angel of light, what authority can the Spirit have with us unless he is identified by an infallible mark? And he is certainly pointed out to us clearly enough by the voice of the Lord; but these wretched men are bent, of their own accord, on going astray to their own ruin, as they seek the Spirit from themselves rather than from Him. But they say that it is an insult to subject the Spirit, to whom all things are to be subject, to Scripture. As if it were a disgrace to the Holy Spirit to be perfectly like himself throughout, and consistent with himself in every respect, without variation! True, if he were held to a human or an angelic standard, or to any standard outside himself, it might be thought that he was being made subordinate, or, if you like, brought into bondage. But so long as he is compared with himself and considered in himself, how can anyone say that he is wronged by this? I admit that he is put to a test, but it is the very test by which he has been pleased to have his majesty confirmed. It ought to be enough for us as soon as we hear his voice; but so that Satan cannot slip in under his name, the Spirit wants us to recognize him by the image he has stamped on the Scriptures. The author of the Scriptures cannot vary or change his likeness. As he showed himself there at first, so he must always remain. There is nothing insulting to him in this—unless we are to think it would be an honor for him to degenerate and revolt against himself.

3\. Their quibble that we cling to the letter that kills carries with it the punishment they deserve for despising Scripture. It is clear that Paul is there arguing against false apostles (2 Cor 3:6) who, by commending the law without Christ, deprived the people of the benefit of the New Covenant, in which the Lord pledges that he will write his law on the hearts of believers and engrave it on their inward parts. The letter, then, is dead, and the law of the Lord kills its readers when it is torn away from the grace of Christ and only sounds in the ear without touching the heart. But if it is effectually impressed on the heart by the Spirit, if it presents Christ, it is the word of life, converting the soul and making wise the simple. Indeed, in the very same passage the apostle calls his own preaching the ministry of the Spirit (2 Cor 3:8), implying that the Holy Spirit so cleaves to his own truth, as he has expressed it in Scripture, that he puts forth and exerts his strength only when the word is received with due honor and respect.

There is nothing here that conflicts with what was said a little earlier#footnote[Ch. 7. (Beveridge’s note.)], that we have no great certainty of the word itself until it is confirmed by the testimony of the Spirit. For the Lord has, by a kind of mutual bond, so knit together the certainty of his word and of his Spirit that our minds are rightly filled with reverence for the word when the Spirit, shining on it, enables us to behold the face of God there; and, on the other hand, we embrace the Spirit with no danger of being deluded when we recognize him in his image, that is, in his word. So indeed it is. God did not bring his word before men for the sake of a momentary display, meaning to abolish it the moment the Spirit arrived. Instead, he employed the same Spirit by whose power he had administered the word to complete his work by the efficacious confirmation of the word. In this way Christ opened the understanding of the two disciples (Luke 24:27), not so that they might throw away the Scriptures and trust in their own wisdom, but so that they might understand the Scriptures. Likewise, when Paul says to the Thessalonians, “Do not quench the Spirit,” he does not sweep them aloft into empty speculation apart from the word; he immediately adds, “Do not despise prophesying” (1 Thess 5:19, 20). By this he undoubtedly implies that the light of the Spirit is quenched the moment prophesying falls into contempt. What answer do those puffed-up fanatics give to this? In their view the only true illumination is to lay aside the Word of God carelessly and bid it farewell, while they seize, with no less confidence than folly, on any dreamed-up notion that may have sprung up by chance in their minds. Surely a very different sobriety befits the children of God. Just as they feel that without the Spirit of God they are utterly without the light of truth, so they are well aware that the word is the instrument by which the Lord dispenses the illumination of his Spirit to believers. They know no other Spirit than the one who dwelt and spoke in the apostles—the Spirit whose oracles daily call them to hear the word.
